00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:15.360
We want to highlight three ideas that are easy to miss when you first start exploring the concept of dataspaces and touch on what it might take to build a dataspace.

00:03:17.670 --> 00:03:22.670
So we're going to start with a real but imagined problem, right?

00:03:22.670 --> 00:03:33.670
So I don't know what the latest is in this sector, but let's pretend that foot and mouth disease is detected near Darwin.

00:03:33.670 --> 00:03:45.210
Now, foot and mouth disease is a biosecurity threat that could arrive in Australia and infect cattle.

00:03:45.210 --> 00:03:55.210
It's highly infectious, and it would have huge trade implications if it was detected in Australia.

00:03:55.210 --> 00:04:04.210
It has the potential to shut down all cattle exports from Australia if it is here and spread widely.

00:04:04.210 --> 00:04:18.210
So in order to contain it, responders would urgently need to know where the cattle are, where this disease is showing up, how cattle might be moving, so where it might be spreading to.

00:04:18.210 --> 00:04:28.210
And it's a national biosecurity issue. So think of it as something on the scale of COVID but happening to the cattle industry.

00:04:29.210 --> 00:04:34.210
So let's imagine foot and mouth disease arrives somewhere near Darwin.

00:04:34.210 --> 00:04:50.840
And if that did happen today, the chances are that we wouldn't have a shared understanding of where livestock are in the country.

00:04:50.840 --> 00:04:54.840
So where are all the cattle currently, and where are they moving?

00:04:54.840 --> 00:05:00.840
We wouldn't have a snapshot ability to pull all that data together quickly.

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:05.840
And we wouldn't be able to share it between the jurisdictions and the Commonwealth and others.

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:14.840
So often these data sets are locked away with industry, so individual producers might have some of it.

00:05:14.840 --> 00:05:19.840
Some of the agencies might have some of it, the departments, some of the jurisdictions, the Commonwealth.

00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:28.840
Again, there's a lot of piecemeal data that is out there, and the cattle trucks and other movement data.

00:05:28.840 --> 00:05:34.840
That data might be available somewhere, but it's not consolidated. It's not easy to share.

00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:44.840
And there's really no standing mechanism to allow people to start sharing that data at scale if something like this were to happen.

00:05:44.840 --> 00:06:00.840
So right now, it would be a real scramble. Think about how public health agencies and hospitals had to sort of scramble to understand where COVID was and where it was spreading.

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:14.840
It was very on the fly working out agreements and sending data around, but it sort of delayed the effectiveness of understanding spread.

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:23.280
And ideally, that's not what we would want to happen if a similar thing worked to impact the cattle industry.

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:39.280
And I guess while you're thinking about this idea of foot and mouth disease arriving in Darwin, I want you to have a think about what's the first thing you would want to happen in this example in order to get the data flowing.

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:57.280
So would you have worked all of this data sharing out beforehand? And if so, what kinds of steps would you initially have started to take to get the data flowing quickly when an emergency like this arises?

00:06:57.280 --> 00:07:09.620
So I just want you to have that idea in the back of your mind. What would you do first if foot and mouth disease arrived in northern Australia?

00:07:09.620 --> 00:07:18.380
But before we go any further, I just want to highlight what is a dataspace? What are we talking about?

00:07:19.380 --> 00:07:36.380
And in one sentence, and I'm just going to read this, a dataspace is digital infrastructure that lets participants find access and use data under the agreed governance framework of that dataspace.

00:07:36.380 --> 00:07:42.380
So restricted access can be shared while providers keep control.

00:07:43.380 --> 00:08:02.340
So it's data sharing of sensitive data done at scale and made very easy. So it's machine actionable data sharing. And it's usually a decentralized system.

00:08:02.340 --> 00:08:14.340
So instead of bringing all the data into one aggregator or one central database, you're just connecting data from various repositories around the place.

00:08:14.340 --> 00:08:28.340
So I guess the important thing to keep in mind here is a dataspace is not just that technological solution. It's the whole picture. It's a process.

00:08:28.340 --> 00:08:45.340
So dataspaces is a years long process, an ongoing process that includes governance. It includes how are we going to deal with all the legal considerations, the policy considerations, the security considerations.

00:08:45.340 --> 00:08:53.340
How are we going to deal with all of that? So a dataspace isn't just the technology that shifts data around. It's the whole picture.

00:08:53.340 --> 00:09:09.970
And there's four pillars that a dataspace is sort of built around. And the first one is identity. So first you have to prove who is who, who's trying to access the data.

00:09:09.970 --> 00:09:15.970
Are they who they say they are? But also there might be a whole lot of other attributes that need to be proven.

00:09:15.970 --> 00:09:30.970
So it's not just the identity of the users, but it can be all kinds of other attributes that need to be proven before data can be allowed to flow between two entities.

00:09:30.970 --> 00:09:38.970
Trustworthiness is a huge part of the dataspace, why it works, the dataspace framework.

00:09:38.970 --> 00:09:53.970
And it's a shared framework that allows participants to understand how data is going to be moved within that individual dataspace. So we'll touch more on that later.

00:09:53.970 --> 00:09:58.970
But trustworthiness is a huge thing and it takes a long time to develop that.

00:09:59.970 --> 00:10:12.970
And data sovereignty is probably one of the biggest selling points of dataspaces. So each provider, so there might be multiple providers in your dataspace who all have different bits of data.

00:10:12.970 --> 00:10:24.970
Each one of those providers can completely decide who they're going to share data with, how much of their data are they going to share, when are they going to share it, for how long are they going to share it.

00:10:24.970 --> 00:10:32.970
All of the conditions around data sharing, and if they share it all, is totally up to the provider.

00:10:32.970 --> 00:10:47.970
So there's this umbrella agreement that all the providers have to come together on. But under that umbrella, individual providers can do whatever they want in terms of how they share data.

00:10:47.970 --> 00:11:00.970
So that's a big selling point of dataspaces. And one of the things that I'm most excited about in dataspaces is that it allows for unprecedented interoperability with sensitive data.

00:11:00.970 --> 00:11:22.970
So if you set up a dataspace, for example, in the biosecurity sector, and you set up another dataspace in the health sector, data from those two dataspaces will be able to move across to one another much more easily than if they weren't dataspaces.

00:11:22.970 --> 00:11:37.970
So there's a standardized process that both the machines and all the legal agreements and everything else have in place, so it's much easier to start sharing data between different domains.

00:11:37.970 --> 00:11:41.970
So that's one of the things that we get excited about at ARDC.

00:11:44.540 --> 00:12:08.540
And when I talk about this umbrella rule, rulebook, it's these agreed rules where all of the people who are participating in a dataspace or all of the organizations will have representation in working out this community agreed set of rules.

00:12:08.540 --> 00:12:30.540
So all the participants understand how it's going to work. So that's a big part of the process of setting up a dataspace. The technology is simply the way in which software and hardware are developed to make sure that all of those rules are carried out technically.

00:12:31.540 --> 00:12:48.540
But the big part of a dataspace is, how do we agree on all these rules with a bunch of different participants? And importantly, so you don't think of a dataspace as a bit of software that you buy and you install it, something you grab off the shelf and you run with.

00:12:48.540 --> 00:12:55.900
It is not that. It is the whole process of sharing sensitive data.

00:12:55.900 --> 00:13:08.900
So I wanted to highlight three things that when we first started talking to stakeholders about dataspaces, they didn't fully understand.

00:13:08.900 --> 00:13:21.900
And I think these are fairly novel to dataspaces, but three things that not everybody understood. So we want to make sure from here forward everybody understands these three concepts.

00:13:21.900 --> 00:13:44.900
The first one is the governance comes before the technology, rules before tools. And the reason rules before tools is important is because you can set a whole bunch of different rules, a wide variety of things that you can set up while you're setting up a dataspace.

00:13:44.900 --> 00:14:00.900
But those agreements might point to very different technological solutions. So you can't really build a dataspace until you know what kinds of specifications need to be met by the participants in that dataspace.

00:14:00.900 --> 00:14:22.900
So rules come first. And the kinds of things you have to agree on are who can participate? How are we going to establish trust? What kinds of conditions need to be in place to say, okay, well, I can shift the data to your organization if the following conditions are met.

00:14:22.900 --> 00:14:34.900
Right? That's how you establish trust is you have technology and rules and legal and policy all understood by all the parties involved.

00:14:34.900 --> 00:14:44.900
How can the data be used in a dataspace? How is compliance going to be checked? Right? So how are we going to make sure everybody's doing what they said they were going to do?

00:14:44.900 --> 00:14:52.900
What if they don't do what they said they were going to do? What if there's a breach? Are there fines? Are there legal ramifications?

00:14:52.900 --> 00:15:06.900
And the rulebook that you're agreeing on is this living document. So it's something that can change over time. How often is it going to be updated? Who's going to update it?

00:15:06.900 --> 00:15:21.900
All of these things are worked out by all of the participants, representatives from all of the organizations that are sharing data coming to an agreement on what these global rules are going to look like.

00:15:21.900 --> 00:15:36.410
And so if we go back to that example of foot and mouth disease in Darwin, it is very difficult to negotiate all of this while the emergency is happening.

00:15:37.410 --> 00:15:48.410
Oftentimes you might start sharing data, but you're doing it in a suboptimal way. You're breaching all kinds of security protocols.

00:15:48.410 --> 00:15:59.410
It's very difficult to even find who all the providers might be for the kinds of data that you need to optimally respond to an outbreak like this.

00:15:59.410 --> 00:16:11.410
So keep in mind the technology can be brought together fairly quickly, but what takes time is these agreements about how are we going to get this data to flow?

00:16:11.410 --> 00:16:24.410
What kinds of conditions need to be in place? Who needs to be a participant in this? You know, you might have veterinarians, you might have operational staff who are driving around to different cattle stations.

00:16:24.410 --> 00:16:48.410
You might have people who are doing other surveillance work. You might have the various jurisdictions, researchers who are trying to model how the disease is most likely spreading, and those researchers will need the latest data in order to come up with the best projections of where this thing might have spread out away from Darwin.

00:16:48.410 --> 00:17:02.410
So all of these things need to be shared relatively quickly, but it can't happen at the drop of a hat because these things take time to negotiate.

00:17:02.410 --> 00:17:21.410
So there's five kinds of dimensions of building this rulebook. So the first thing that people who are thinking about creating a dataspace need to come to agreement on is what's the business case for me participating in this dataspace?

00:17:21.410 --> 00:17:36.410
So how am I going to get value by participating in this dataspace? What's in it for me? And all of the participants need to come to an understanding, whether they're a data provider or a data consumer.

00:17:36.410 --> 00:17:43.410
All of them need to understand, well, this is why sharing data would be valuable to me.

00:17:43.410 --> 00:18:01.410
Then we need to check off on all of the legal constraints. There's a lot of legal constraints around biosecurity data, sharing data between jurisdictions and the Commonwealth, privacy concerns, other policy concerns that need to be taken into account.

00:18:01.410 --> 00:18:09.410
Legal plays a big part in understanding what can and cannot be shared and under what conditions.

00:18:09.410 --> 00:18:28.410
And then the group of dataspace participants need to agree on who's going to manage the day-to-day operation of the dataspace. Is it going to be a group of us? How are we going to fund that group that's going to do that work?

00:18:28.410 --> 00:18:38.410
Or are we going to contract it out? All of these things. How are we going to operationalize the running of the dataspace?

00:18:38.410 --> 00:18:46.410
And importantly, what does this dataspace ultimately have to do? So obviously, it needs to push data from one person to another.

00:18:46.410 --> 00:19:00.410
But what are the other things that it needs to do? Does it need to be able to push data into an analytics platform where nobody can see the data, but we get an understanding of where foot and mouth disease might be spreading?

00:19:00.410 --> 00:19:10.410
What kinds of functional things does this dataspace need to be able to do in order to deliver value for all of those participants?

00:19:10.410 --> 00:19:29.410
And then the technical is all of the standards and all of the software that implements all of these rules. So those are the five things that you need to think about when you're setting up a dataspace. And again, it's not a small thing.

00:19:29.410 --> 00:19:40.410
So there's a big upfront cost to setting up a dataspace. But once you kind of agree on the rules and you've captured them in something called a rulebook, and that rulebook might change over time.

00:19:40.410 --> 00:19:53.410
But then once you have the rulebook set, then you can build the solution to make sure all of those things specified in the rulebook are taken care of by the technology.

00:19:53.410 --> 00:20:01.410
And once that happens, it becomes very easy to share. Maybe you're only going to share data when the emergency hits.

00:20:01.410 --> 00:20:08.410
But when the emergency does hit, it's easy to share the data that needs to be shared to whomever.

00:20:08.410 --> 00:20:22.410
And often once you start sharing sensitive data, you find that there are other organizations or other groups that you can also see value in starting to share some of that data with other parties.

00:20:22.410 --> 00:20:43.410
So it starts to scale data sharing of sensitive data, and that process is very easy, much, much easier at least, than trying to negotiate bilateral data sharing agreements or whatever the current process might be.

00:20:43.410 --> 00:20:48.410
So it becomes very repeatable, very easy to do that.

00:20:49.410 --> 00:21:04.410
So that was the first thing that wasn't understood, is that there's this community effort to build the rulebook, and only after you build the rulebook with all of the participants engaged in that can you build a dataspace.

00:21:04.410 --> 00:21:15.410
The second thing that we found a lot of stakeholders missed was this idea that the technology has two types of things going on.

00:21:15.410 --> 00:21:23.410
And one is called the control plane, and one is called the data plane. And they're very different things.

00:21:23.410 --> 00:21:39.410
So the control plane, that coordinates all of the rules of data exchange. So it takes care of negotiating contracts, it takes care of identifying

00:21:39.410 --> 00:21:53.410
if the person who is trying to access data has all of the necessary attributes to actually be able to see that data. How are we going to log these contracts?

00:21:53.410 --> 00:22:01.410
All of that takes place in the control plane. Unfortunately, there's a lot of standards that are built around that.

00:22:01.410 --> 00:22:12.410
So there's not a lot of flexibility in how you specify these things, but what you specify is wide open. So again, the rules can vary quite a lot.

00:22:12.410 --> 00:22:25.410
But from a technical standpoint, there are standards and vocabularies that you have to use in the control plane so that two connectors can talk to each other, and we'll talk about connectors in a second.

00:22:26.410 --> 00:22:34.410
So control plane, highly standardized, but flexible in terms of what you can enact within that.

00:22:34.410 --> 00:22:49.410
The data plane, you can do whatever you want in the data plane, essentially. So the data plane just handles the actual transfer of the data, and we'll talk more about what that might look like, but it's very, very flexible.

00:22:49.410 --> 00:22:57.410
So you have a very standardized control plane, and you have a very flexible data plane.

00:22:57.410 --> 00:23:14.410
So in the control plane, what does the control plane comprise of? So primarily, one of the key aspects of the control plane is this thing called the dataspace protocol, which is essentially a communications protocol so that

00:23:14.410 --> 00:23:26.410
our machines in different organizations are speaking the same language. And so when they go and ask for data, the other machine knows what it's asking for, because it's asking for it in the right way.

00:23:26.410 --> 00:23:36.410
And then it can say, oh, well, here's an offer, and here's a contract. And all of those things, if they're standardized, the machines can just do them automatically.

00:23:36.410 --> 00:23:47.410
So it's the way you talk about contract negotiation that is standardized so that any organization within the dataspace can talk to one another.

00:23:47.410 --> 00:23:58.410
And so if you're in one dataspace, you can talk to another dataspace. So it's all of those standards, and we'll talk about that a little bit more later as well.

00:23:58.410 --> 00:24:18.410
The control plane has to identify not only is the person who they say they are, are they from the organization they say they are, but there might be all kinds of other attributes that they also need to verify before data will be shared between those two organizations.

00:24:18.410 --> 00:24:29.410
All of this has to be machine readable and machine actionable. So you've got to express all of these things in written code, essentially.

00:24:29.410 --> 00:24:45.410
And then finally, you have the contracts that are negotiated and logged. So every single piece of data that is exchanged through a dataspace gets a contract.

00:24:45.410 --> 00:24:55.410
And that might sound like a lot of work, but all of that happens automatically. So the control plane are where the standards do the work.

00:24:55.410 --> 00:25:10.380
And within the control plane, the primary differentiator within a dataspace, the thing that makes dataspaces most novel is this thing called a connector.

00:25:10.380 --> 00:25:19.380
And a connector is the software that implements contract negotiation and usage control.

00:25:19.380 --> 00:25:25.380
So it's the software that every participant needs to be able to run.

00:25:25.380 --> 00:25:38.380
And think of it like the security guard that stands outside your organization's door who's going to check to make sure, okay, the person knocking on the door is who they say they are.

00:25:38.380 --> 00:25:48.380
And we're going to implement all of these rules, the contract negotiation within the control plane.

00:25:48.380 --> 00:25:58.380
We're going to check on who they are. So all of the things that are happening in the control plane are being done by this connector, which is a bundle of software.

00:25:58.380 --> 00:26:02.380
And that bundle of software wraps around the data plane.

00:26:02.380 --> 00:26:08.380
So again, anything you can push and pull data any way you want through the data plane.

00:26:08.380 --> 00:26:20.660
But the connector is the security guard that makes sure everything happens as it's supposed to, as it was specified by that rulebook.

00:26:20.660 --> 00:26:32.660
So when we're thinking about identity, most of us are familiar with an identity provider that offers role based access controls, right?

00:26:32.660 --> 00:26:40.660
So it's going to verify you are who you say you are, you are from the organization you say you're from.

00:26:40.660 --> 00:26:45.660
The Australian Access Federation does a lot of this work for most universities.

00:26:45.660 --> 00:26:58.660
When you log into a platform, there might be a verification step to verify that you are working on a university computer.

00:26:58.660 --> 00:27:11.660
Your email has been verified as the one that's logged into that computer, and you therefore are able to access that particular service.

00:27:11.660 --> 00:27:17.660
So most of us are familiar with those kinds of role based access controls.

00:27:17.660 --> 00:27:22.660
But again, in a dataspace, identity is much more than a name.

00:27:22.660 --> 00:27:33.950
There's a whole lot of other components to be verified, a whole lot of other attributes that need to be verified.

00:27:33.950 --> 00:27:41.950
And let's think about the contract negotiation that's part of the dataspace protocol.

00:27:41.950 --> 00:27:46.950
So the dataspace protocol has these two connectors that are talking to each other.

00:27:46.950 --> 00:27:49.950
So one, you discover the data set, then you request the data set.

00:27:49.950 --> 00:27:54.950
The other connector will negotiate some terms.

00:27:54.950 --> 00:28:04.950
Then the other connector will confirm your identities, and both connectors will verify that all the identities,

00:28:04.950 --> 00:28:11.950
all the attributes that are being looked for are indeed being met by this organization.

00:28:11.950 --> 00:28:15.950
Then you're going to agree to the contract, and that goes back and forth.

00:28:15.950 --> 00:28:20.950
And then finally, you're going to give it a green light and say, okay, we can now transfer the data.

00:28:20.950 --> 00:28:24.950
So the security guard can wave the data through, right?

00:28:24.950 --> 00:28:30.950
So all of that needs to happen within each connector.

00:28:30.950 --> 00:28:35.950
And the dataspace protocol ensures that all of your machines are talking the same language.

00:28:35.950 --> 00:28:37.950
They're talking to each other well.

00:28:37.950 --> 00:28:51.840
And one of the really hard parts about all of this is taking a policy around appropriate data use.

00:28:51.840 --> 00:28:54.840
Say you need to delete the data after 30 days.

00:28:54.840 --> 00:28:59.840
We're going to give it to you for 30 days, but you can't hang on to it for longer than that.

00:28:59.840 --> 00:29:05.310
And there might be a whole lot of other things that are within that policy.

00:29:05.310 --> 00:29:13.310
To turn that policy into machine-readable and machine-actionable code takes a lot of work.

00:29:13.310 --> 00:29:19.310
So one way that it's often done is using Open Digital Rights Language (ODRL).

00:29:19.310 --> 00:29:23.310
And essentially, this is a World Wide Web standard.

00:29:23.310 --> 00:29:32.310
So it translates this plain English policy into something that a machine can read in action.

00:29:32.310 --> 00:29:34.310
But it's hard to write a good one.

00:29:34.310 --> 00:29:41.310
So we're lucky in the dataspaces community, dataspaces have been going on for 10 years in Europe.

00:29:41.310 --> 00:29:50.310
And they have some off-the-shelf policies that you can grab, between 18 and 23 of them, depending on which connectors you're using.

00:29:50.310 --> 00:29:54.310
But those have been certified.

00:29:54.310 --> 00:29:58.310
Those do what the policy says they will do.

00:29:58.310 --> 00:30:08.310
It's hard to make sure that your connector policy does exactly what it says on the tin.

00:30:08.310 --> 00:30:17.310
So it takes a lot of work to certify that your policy written code is doing exactly what it says on the tin.

00:30:17.310 --> 00:30:29.310
So you can implement policies that aren't off-the-shelf ready for you, but just keep in mind it's going to take some cost and some time to get that done.

00:30:29.310 --> 00:30:35.310
If you have a policy that's different from the 18 to 23 that are already available.

00:30:35.310 --> 00:30:47.860
So if we think about the data plane, the data plane is able to do whatever you want.

00:30:47.860 --> 00:30:59.860
So, again, the data might go from one participant to another through an API access or some kind of file transfer, but there might be a secure container involved.

00:30:59.860 --> 00:31:03.860
You might be pushing data into a trusted research environment.

00:31:03.860 --> 00:31:10.860
You might be pushing data into an analytics platform, a Databricks solution.

00:31:10.860 --> 00:31:18.860
You might be using a secure vault where nobody can actually, you push the data into the secure vault, but nobody sees it.

00:31:18.860 --> 00:31:31.180
And you can't take it out of the secure vault, but you can do analysis on those data and take out the data products.

00:31:31.180 --> 00:31:36.180
All kinds of things that you can do in the data plane. It's totally up to you.

00:31:36.180 --> 00:31:43.180
A lot of those solutions are not available out of the box from Europe, but you can do whatever you want to do.

00:31:43.180 --> 00:31:47.180
Some of them just might be a little more costly than others.

00:31:47.180 --> 00:31:56.180
You can also do something called federated learning. So you can actually bring the compute to the data.

00:31:56.180 --> 00:32:07.180
So essentially you set up an analysis and you gather all of the bits of information that you need from each of the organization's databases.

00:32:07.180 --> 00:32:13.180
And then you pull that information together to get your final result.

00:32:13.180 --> 00:32:30.620
So if we think about the foot and mouth disease example, so you might have different producers that have different amount of data on where their data, where their cows are.

00:32:33.220 --> 00:32:58.220
They don't want to give you access to the raw data, but they'll give you access to be able to say, well, what's the incidence rate and the detection rates and the surveillance rates of looking for this disease in the various stations around the Northern Territory.

00:32:58.220 --> 00:33:11.220
So they're not going to give you all the data on the raw cows, but they might be able to give you summary results that you can then pull together for a global picture of what surveillance and spread is looking like at the moment.

00:33:11.220 --> 00:33:24.220
So federated learning is an increasingly popular solution. It's probably the most secure. Your data never actually leaves your organization.

00:33:24.220 --> 00:33:37.220
You just pull the required summary data out of those raw data, but it's not an out of the box solution. So it is a little bit more costly to implement.

00:33:37.220 --> 00:33:43.220
There are some people in Australia who are doing this kind of work. QCIF is one of those.

00:33:43.220 --> 00:33:56.340
And at a glance, there's some essential components in a dataspace.

00:33:56.340 --> 00:34:03.340
And at ARDC, I think we'd argue that even the optional ones are a really good idea, but the mandatory ones are you need a connector.

00:34:03.340 --> 00:34:14.340
Every organization who's participating, whether you're a data provider or a data consumer, needs this data connector, which is the bit of software, the security guard.

00:34:14.340 --> 00:34:20.340
You need an identity provider. You need a way to prove the attributes that you're looking for.

00:34:20.340 --> 00:34:28.340
You need some sort of policy enforcement mechanisms, and you need a data source and a data destination.

00:34:28.340 --> 00:34:35.880
So where are you moving data from and to? Usually, you need a catalog.

00:34:36.880 --> 00:34:49.880
So you need somewhere where somebody can log in and the catalog might have access control. So you might have to have a certain credential or be from a certain organization to even see the catalog.

00:34:49.880 --> 00:34:57.880
But once you're in the catalog, you should be able to see all of the data that's available from the various organizations.

00:34:57.880 --> 00:35:06.130
Usually, you're going to want a clearinghouse. A clearinghouse tracks all of the things that are going on in your dataspace.

00:35:06.130 --> 00:35:19.130
It gives you an audit trail, and it gives you a way for someone to look and verify that all of the things that we agree to are actually happening within the dataspace.

00:35:19.130 --> 00:35:29.440
Often, there's something called an app store. So an app store is just an application that sits on top of a dataspace.

00:35:29.440 --> 00:35:44.440
And it will do extra things. So an analytics platform would be an example of an app that would also have to have connectors, but could pull data together to do analyses.

00:35:44.440 --> 00:35:54.440
So a lot of the exciting things happen in an app store, an application that sits on top of the dataspace that does something downstream of just the raw data.

00:35:55.440 --> 00:36:10.440
And usually, so within the dataspace protocol, there'll be some vocabularies that you have to use, but often there'll be some domain-specific vocabularies that you will want to use so everybody understands the data that's being shared.

00:36:13.650 --> 00:36:31.650
So the third concept that we want to come back to is this idea, and that a lot of people missed, and I've already alluded to it a few times, is trust isn't just based on who you are, but it's also based on what you can prove.

00:36:31.650 --> 00:36:51.650
So for example, traditionally, and we've talked about this, AAF would identify who you are and what organization you're from, but there might be a whole lot of other attributes that you want to prove before you start to share data with another organization.

00:36:51.650 --> 00:37:03.650
So that could be, for example, you might want to prove that, okay, the organization is who they say they are, they are from the state that we're working in, they're from the jurisdiction of the Northern Territory.

00:37:03.650 --> 00:37:13.650
They actually are a veterinarian with authority to do surveillance for foot and mouth disease, so the role and the authority to act.

00:37:14.650 --> 00:37:29.650
You might want information on their certification status, so their connector is a five-star connector. The organization that's asking for data in this dataspace has kept their connector up to date, and they've even certified it to a level of five stars.

00:37:30.650 --> 00:37:48.650
You might want to understand the security posture of the organization that you're sharing data with. Have they gone through the process of becoming, their data becoming ISO 27001 certified, and to what level have they gone through that process?

00:37:49.650 --> 00:37:57.650
There might be professional accreditations, so somebody who's in the field doing operations understands foot and mouth disease really well.

00:37:58.650 --> 00:38:08.650
If they do, and they've gone through the requisite trainings, they could have access to that data. So there's all kinds of things that you might have to prove aside from just, I am who I say I am.

00:38:12.140 --> 00:38:30.140
And again, if we think about responders on the ground, often people who are trying to understand how this disease might be spreading need the movement data right away, but they can't get that with a generic login.

00:38:31.140 --> 00:38:39.140
Again, they might have to prove that they're an accredited veterinary officer with an understanding of animal disease.

00:38:42.140 --> 00:38:57.900
They have the authority to act during this emergency, right? So all of these things, if they're set up ahead of time, can be scaled really quickly and easily.

00:38:58.900 --> 00:39:04.900
So once the front end cost is there, once the dataspace is set up, all of this data gets pushed around very quickly.

00:39:05.900 --> 00:39:24.310
So one of the other things in a dataspace that is a little bit unique is all of these claims, all of these attributes need to be proven, and there are organizations that manage the way those things are proven.

00:39:25.310 --> 00:39:43.310
So we've talked about AAF, the Australian Access Federation, so they do a lot of this for universities, proving you are an individual with the right email address and the right two-factor authentications with a university computer.

00:39:44.310 --> 00:40:03.310
Those organizations that do this work are called trust anchors. And so again, they might reach, trust anchors might be certified bodies. So IDSA could say, yes, your connector is a top grade connector. It's at the highest level of certification.

00:40:04.310 --> 00:40:24.310
There might be other certification authorities that manage different kinds of certifications. There are government and industry registers. So yes, this person is from New South Wales or Queensland and working in the correct government department.

00:40:25.310 --> 00:40:41.310
There might be standard bodies. So people who have come up with the vocabularies of how we're going to talk about foot and mouth disease, right? We're all going to talk about it in the same way. When I say surveillance, this is what I mean. When I say detection, this is what I mean.

00:40:41.310 --> 00:41:09.310
And I'm always going to report what the sensitivity of that test is. So there's all kinds of things that might need to be verified before data starts to flow. So everybody needs to be talking about surveillance in the same way before we're going to start sharing data about surveillance, so there's no misunderstandings, right?

00:41:12.750 --> 00:41:27.750
I just want to briefly touch on what it takes to build a dataspace. So hopefully I've touched on what a dataspace is and what some of the things that early stakeholders that we were explaining dataspace is often missed.

00:41:28.750 --> 00:41:52.540
But to build one, again, you have to come up with this group that have worked together that represent all of the organizations that are wanting to either provide data or consume data, and they've come together to understand who, what, and how all of this is going to work.

00:41:53.540 --> 00:42:18.530
And it's that group effort that sort of builds trust. So coming to an understanding of all of these parts of how we're going to set up a rulebook, coming to those understandings builds trust because we understand exactly what needs to be in place before I'm willing to share data.

00:42:19.530 --> 00:42:42.350
And this is just an indication. This is a guest by Muhammad, who's also at ARDC and myself. We were sort of throwing out some figures, so these might not be totally accurate. Again, we're fairly new to dataspaces in Australia. We might find that things cost more or less than this.

00:42:42.350 --> 00:43:10.350
But it looks like if we were just going to set up a demonstrator in your domain to demonstrate how data can flow from a provider to a consumer and meet a number of policy obligations, just that demonstrator, that software that allows data to go from one database to another would cost upwards of $150,000.

00:43:12.350 --> 00:43:24.350
If we were going to have a limited production dataspace where you have one consumer and one provider, that could be upwards of $1 to $1.5 million.

00:43:24.350 --> 00:43:35.350
We've got a whole bunch of organizations that are starting to share data at scale, and it's a fully production ready dataspace

00:43:35.350 --> 00:43:50.980
that could cost anywhere from $2 to $5 million. And in the case of biosecurity or finance or health, if the data is highly sensitive, those costs could even go up, right?

00:43:50.980 --> 00:44:02.980
And just coming back to the foot and mouth disease example, I don't think we have enough resource to pull that off in the biosecurity sector in the next year, for example.

00:44:02.980 --> 00:44:10.980
I just don't think we have enough resource to get all of the players involved and starting to share data.

00:44:10.980 --> 00:44:20.980
So in animal biosecurity, it might make more sense to start off with a real obvious use case where you have a data provider and a data consumer.

00:44:21.980 --> 00:44:33.980
And you know that there would be a lot of value if they were pushing and pulling data between one another and work on that smaller use case before you take on the whole biosecurity sector, right?

00:44:33.980 --> 00:44:40.790
Because that's going to be a much more expensive operation.

00:44:40.790 --> 00:45:00.790
So again, you start with a shared problem, you identify what's the problem that we're trying to solve. In the case of foot and mouth disease, we want to be prepared so we can identify all of the locations where foot and mouth disease has spread to and contain it as quickly as possible.

00:45:00.790 --> 00:45:09.790
So it doesn't spread throughout Australia and shut down exports of cattle, right, or cattle products.

00:45:09.790 --> 00:45:13.790
And that takes some real commitment to do that ahead of time.

00:45:13.790 --> 00:45:23.790
But if the value is there, you're going to likely agree that, okay, we understand that there's some short-term costs to make this happen.

00:45:23.790 --> 00:45:31.790
But when an emergency happens, the data is going to be able to be flowing as needed, right?

00:45:31.790 --> 00:45:41.190
So here in Australia, again, ARDC has been at this for a little over a year.

00:45:41.190 --> 00:45:49.190
And we've had a couple of pilots where we've learned a lot about dataspaces.

00:45:50.190 --> 00:45:57.190
We've learned about the concepts, but also how to describe the concepts.

00:45:57.190 --> 00:46:11.630
A testbed has been built, so it's a place where you can demo the technology and start to explore whether what you've built meets different standards.

00:46:11.630 --> 00:46:16.630
So the IDSA, the testbed is in place.

00:46:16.630 --> 00:46:29.100
And generally, we're now moving on to phase two where we're looking to actually build a couple of these dataspaces in the next year and a half or so.

00:46:29.100 --> 00:46:36.100
Globally, there's over 200 dataspaces that are being developed. Some of those are quite mature.

00:46:36.100 --> 00:46:46.100
So Catena-X is an example of a very mature dataspace. The mobility dataspace is another example where you started out with limited data sharing,

00:46:46.100 --> 00:46:49.100
but that has grown to many different industries.

00:46:49.100 --> 00:47:01.100
So the mobility dataspace is including insurance companies and automobile manufacturers and local city jurisdictions and countries.

00:47:01.100 --> 00:47:10.100
And so all of these different data sets and all of these players are starting to share data at scale.

00:47:11.100 --> 00:47:18.100
And all of these are backed by standards. It's all backed by open source data.

00:47:18.100 --> 00:47:24.100
So you can find connectors online and use those.

00:47:24.100 --> 00:47:27.100
So there's lots and lots of tools.

00:47:27.100 --> 00:47:38.100
Because this has been happening for 10 years in Europe, there's almost nothing that you could think about regarding sharing sensitive data

00:47:38.100 --> 00:47:44.100
that hasn't been thought about in excruciating detail by the Europeans over the last 10 years.

00:47:44.100 --> 00:47:53.100
So it's nice because we can borrow a lot of that. The things that we can't borrow are legal and policy things that are specific to Australia that we have to work through.

00:47:53.100 --> 00:48:04.100
But there is so much rich information to draw from that it makes it much easier for us to stand up dataspaces than it would have been for the Europeans originally.

00:48:04.100 --> 00:48:16.450
So just keep in mind that there is a second session on dataspaces and we'll go a little more deeply into some of what we've discussed today.

00:48:16.450 --> 00:48:29.270
But this first session, I just wanted to sort of drive home the idea that what a dataspace is and what are some of the key concepts.

00:48:29.270 --> 00:48:41.270
There's some other resources here on this page that would allow you to learn more. So there's a dataspaces FAQ page on the ARDC website.

00:48:41.270 --> 00:48:56.270
We have something called a dataspace educator, which is a chat bot that's trained on a local RAG architecture. So it's trained on actual dataspace information from IDSA and DSSC.

00:48:56.270 --> 00:49:09.270
And we also have a community page if you want to start getting involved. So there's tons more to learn. Again, usually a dataspaces 101 class is going to run for eight hours.

00:49:09.270 --> 00:49:13.270
So there's a lot to know. We've just sort of skimmed on it.

00:49:13.270 --> 00:49:29.270
But the three things that I want you to take away from today are rules before tools, governance that's a community process of all of the participants who are sharing data have to build those rules.

00:49:29.270 --> 00:49:40.270
Once those rules are in place, that specifies what technology to build. You can't build the technology first because you don't know what to build until the rules are in place.

00:49:41.270 --> 00:49:51.270
The control plane and the data plane are very different things. The control plane has a lot of standards. The data plane you can do almost anything you can imagine in.

00:49:51.270 --> 00:50:06.270
And trust is more what you can prove, not just who you are. So there's a whole bunch of attributes that you might need to prove as part of operating within the dataspace beyond I am who I say I am.

00:50:06.270 --> 00:50:22.270
So those are the things I want you to keep in mind. And if you are able to do a deeper dive, there is tons of information out there. I would encourage you to focus on these four main sources of information.

00:50:22.270 --> 00:50:31.270
So the DSSC dataspaces blueprint is a great place to understand how to get started in building dataspaces.

00:50:31.270 --> 00:50:47.270
The IDSA rulebook gives you an understanding of all of the components that we're talking about today. The Sitra rulebook gives you sort of a step-by-step way of working through the process of developing a rulebook.

00:50:47.270 --> 00:50:58.270
And the IDS reference architecture manual, that's something that your tech team needs to understand, especially your technical architects.

00:50:59.270 --> 00:51:15.270
And that specifies all the technology that's available to make this feasible. All of that is described in the RAM, the reference architecture model.

00:51:15.270 --> 00:51:27.270
So that's it for today. If you want to learn more, we'll have the second session in a separate video. Thank you.

00:51:28.270 --> 00:51:30.270
Thank you.
